{"timestamp": "2026-07-28T09:55:01.392922", "data": {"url": "https://www.tata.com/about-us/tata-group-our-heritage/Our-Timeline", "title": "Our Timeline: The Complete Story | Tata group", "content": "Share 1868 1874 1886 1892 1903 1904 1907 1909 1910 1912 1917 1919 1920 1929 1932 1936 1938 1939 1941 1945 1946 1952 1954 1962 1968 1969 1982 1983 1984 1991 1992 1994 1998 2000 2001 2002 2003 2004 2006 2007 2008 2011 2012 2014 2015 2017 2018 2019 2020 2021 2022 2022 2023 2024 1868 29-year-old Jamsetji Nusserwanji Tata starts a trading company with a capital of \u20b921,000 \u2014 the early beginnings of what would one day become the Tata group. Jamsetji Tata: The Giant Who Touched Tomorrow / 1874 In a bold move, Jamsetji establishes a textile mill in Nagpur instead of Bombay \u2014 India's textile hub. The Empress Mills experiment would prove to be a stroke of genius. Through the Mill / 1886 Empress Mills pioneers employee welfare initiatives, long before they are enacted by law. 150 years later, the Tata group remains a 'people-first' enterprise. People First: Pioneering Worker Welfare / 1892 Jamsetji establishes the JN Tata Endowment Fund to help Indian students pursue higher studies abroad. JN Tata Endowment Fund: Backing The Brightest Read More Freany Cama, the very first recipient of the JN Tata Endowment, was a doctor. / To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that supports HTML5 video 1903 The Taj Mahal Hotel opens for business on December 16. It is one of Jamsetji's many dreams for India, and the only one that would come to fruition in his lifetime. Taj Mahal Palace: Diamond By The Sea / 1904 It falls to his older son, Sir Dorabji Tata, to bring his father's grand vision for India to life. He becomes Chairman when Jamsetji passes away in Germany in 1904. Sir Dorabji Tata: His Father's Son / 1907 Sir Dorab first establishes Tata Iron and Steel Company (now Tata Steel) in remote Sakchi, and builds a hospital for the village, years before the first ingot is rolled out. Nerves Of Steel 1907 Tata Limited, the group's first overseas office, opens in London. The step marks the beginning of the group's global ambitions. Read More Tata Limited team outing at Skindles Hotel in Maidenhead, Berkshire, UK, in 1930. / 1909 The Indian Institute of Science is established through the vision of JN Tata, 5 years after his death. The first batch of students is admitted in 1911. IISc: Science With A Soul / To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that supports HTML5 video 1910 Jamsetji's dream of bringing clean energy to Mumbai by establishing Western India's first hydro plant, is brought to life by Sir Dorab. Tata Power is born. The Power Of Dreams / 1912 Moved by widespread poverty in India, Sir Ratan Tata, Jamsetji's younger son and a philanthropist, funds research into its causes at the University of London. Beyond Business: Sir Ratanji Tata To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that supports HTML5 video 1912 India's first planned industrial city springs to life in Sakchi. In 1919, it is rechristened Jamshedpur to honour Jamsetji, who first envisioned the township. / 1917 The group makes its consumer space debut with Tata Oil Mills Co (TOMCO), known for popular soap brands, Hamam and Moti. It is sold to Hindustan Lever in 1984. / 1919 Sir Ratan Tata passes away this year, and the first of the Tata Trusts, the Sir Ratanji Tata Trust & Allied Trusts, is set up in accordance with his will. It has a corpus of Rs. 8 million. Sir Ratan Tata Trust & Allied Trusts / 1920 India sends her first Olympic team to the games in Antwerp, largely due to Sir Dorab's efforts. In 1924, he finances the Indian team headed to the Paris games. / 1929 24-year-old JRD Tata receives the first pilot licence in India, the culmination of a childhood fascination with flying and with legendary aviator Louis Bleriot. / To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that supports HTML5 video 1932 India's aviation aspirations soar as JRD pilots the first flight of the subcontinent from Karachi to Mumbai. Tata Airlines (later, Air India) is born. Wings For A Nation 1932 Donating all of his wealth (Rs. 10 million at the time), Sir Dorab sets up the Sir Dorabji Tata Trust and the Lady Tata Memorial Trust (in memory of his wife). Sir Dorabji Tata Trust & Allied Trusts 1932 Sir Dorab passes away the same year. His trusted lieutenant and passionate labour advocate Nowroji Saklatvala assumes Chairmanship of the Tata group. A People's Person: Sir Nowroji Saklatvala / 1936 The Tata Institute of Social Sciences is established, pioneering social work education in India. It remains an institution of excellence to this day. Tata Institute of Social Sciences / 1938 Sir Nowroji passes away in France, propelling JRD Tata to the most powerful position in the group. At 34, he is the youngest Chairman in the group's history. Lessons In Leadership: JRD Tata / 1939 Tata Chemicals is incorporated in isolated, coastal Mithapur. A thriving community grows around the plant as the company grows into a market leader. / To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that supports HTML5 video 1941 The Tata Memorial Hospital is commissioned by the Sir Dorabji Tata Trust. It pioneers cancer treatment in the country, and offers access to cancer care for all. Tata Memorial Centre / 1945 The group's rapid business expansion continues with the establishment of Tata Engineering and Locomotive Company. By 2003, it is rechristened Tata Motors. To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that supports HTML5 video 1945 India's scientific infrastructure takes a decisive step forward, when JRD Tata and Homi Bhabha establish the Tata Institute of Fundamental Research. Tata Institute of Fundamental Research / 1946 Naval Tata is named President of the Indian Hockey Association. Under his watch, India achieves Olympic glory, with golds in 1948, 1952 & 1956. Keeper Of The Flame: Naval Tata / To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that supports HTML5 video 1952 Women across the country celebrate as the group launches India's first cosmetics brand, Lakme. It is sold to Hindustan Unilever, along with TOMCO, in 1984. / 1954 Voltas is incorporated and is associated with multiple prestigious projects, making it a leading engineering solutions provider and airconditioning brand. Read More The manufacturing unit set-up in Mumbai, in 1954. 1954 TELCO enters the commercial vehicle space in collaboration with Daimler Benz AG, West Germany. The first Tata Mercedes-Benz truck rolls out in six months. / To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that supports HTML5 video 1962 The group's interest in the beverages space is brewing. The country wakes up to a cup of Tata Finlay (later, the iconic Tata Tea; now, Tata Global Beverages). Read More Tata Tea's popular campaigns have included 'Asli Taazgi' in the '90s, featuring a Bollywood hit, and recently, 'Jaago Re'. To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that supports HTML5 video 1962 Tata Exports (now, Tata International) is set up. The company operates in 39 countries, focusing on trading & distribution, and manufacturing leather products. / 1968 India's first software services company, Tata Consultancy Services ushers in a new era. Today, it is a truly global enterprise with a presence in 46 countries. / 1969 JRD Tata and Dr. Jamshed Bhabha envision a world-class performing arts centre to preserve India's heritage. Enter, the National Centre for the Performing Arts. National Centre For The Performing Arts / 1982 The Taj Group accelerates its international expansion with the acquisition of St. James Court in London, UK, two years after the company's first international hotel in Yemen. / To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that supports HTML5 video 1983 Tata Chemicals changes the way India consumes salt. Tata Salt is the country's first iodised branded salt \u2014 the 'desh ka namak' to build a strong, healthy nation. Read More A Tata Salt campaign promoting its new packaging in 2015. / 1984 The group clocks another win as Titan Industries, a JV with the Tamil Nadu Industrial Development Corporation (TIDCO), makes a splash in a watch-starved market. Read More JRD Tata at the inauguration of Titan's Hosur factory in March 1988. / 1991 After its success with commercial vehicles, TELCO (now Tata Motors) cruises into the passenger vehicle space with Tata Sierra, followed by Tata Estate in 1992. Read More The Tata Sierra marks Tata Motors' foray into the passenger vehicle market. 1991 With a view to preserving the group's unique heritage, JRD Tata establishes the Tata Central Archives in Mumbai. The archives find a new home in Pune in 2001. Treasures From The Past 1991 After 53 years at the helm, JRD Tata steps down as Chairman, naming Ratan N Tata as successor. Under Ratan Tata, the group grows into a $100bn enterprise. / 1992 With an eye on an evolving market, Titan launches Raga to tap into the women's watch segment. Fastrack (launched 1998) becomes an iconic youth brand. Read More A recent campaign for Titan Raga featuring Bollywood star Kriti Sanon. To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that supports HTML5 video 1992 JRD Tata is awarded India's highest civilian honour, the Bharat Ratna, for his contribution to nation building, and also wins the UN Population Award this year. / 1994 Titan revolutionises the Indian jewellery market with the launch of Tanishq. The retail brand brings transparency and reliability to an unorganised sector. Read More A recent festive Tanishq campaign featuring brand ambassador Deepika Padukone. / 1998 Tata Indica, India's first indigenously designed and manufactured car, and Tata Safari, India's first SUV, are launched by TELCO (now, Tata Motors). Read More Ratan Tata drives the first Tata Indica off the assembly line. / To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that supports HTML5 video 2000 Tata Tea (now Tata Global Beverages) acquires the iconic, then 160-year-old, British Tetley group. Today, it is the second largest tea brand, globally. Tetley's 180-Year-Old Journey / 2001 The group strengthens its presence in the insurance sector with two joint ventures \u2014 Tata AIG this year, and Tata AIA in 2000. Read More Tata AIA's 2018 campaign, #LoveUnconditionally. / To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that supports HTML5 video 2002 The Tata group acquires a controlling stake in VSNL, establishing Tata Communications. The company is a global leader in network solutions, mobility and IoT. / 2003 TCS is the first Indian software company to cross one billion dollars in revenues. It goes public in 2004 in the largest IPO in India, raising nearly $1.2bn. Read More Chairman Emeritus Ratan Tata, flanked by then-CEO S. Ramadorai and then-EVP Phiroz Vandrevala, lists TCS on the NSE. / 2004 Tata Motors acquires the heavy vehicles unit of Daewoo Motors, and in the same year, is listed on the New York Stock Exchange. / 2006 The group responds to the transforming satellite entertainment space by launching DTH (Direct-to-Home) provider, Tata Sky. Read More A recent Tata Sky campaign featuring South Indian cinema star Nayanthara. 2006 Infiniti Retail launches Croma, India's first national chain of multi brand outlets for consumer electronics and durables. / 2007 Tata Steel acquires Corus (now, Tata Steel Europe). It is Europe's second largest steel producer serving construction, automotive, packaging & engineering industries. / To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that supports HTML5 video 2008 Tata Motors unveils the 'people's car', Tata Nano, to make safe transport affordable to millions. It also acquires iconic British car maker, Jaguar Land Rover. / 2011 Tata Medical Center, a comprehensive cancer care and treatment facility designed especially to serve the poor in the region, is inaugurated by Ratan Tata. Tata Medical Center / 2012 TGBL's JV with international coffee giant, Starbucks, is welcomed by coffee lovers. The next 5 years see the fastest rollout of stores in Starbucks' history. Starbucks Partners: Brewing A Unique Culture / 2014 Bombay House, is the first heritage building in India to receive a gold rating by Indian Green Building Council. Two years later, it receives a platinum rating. 2014 The group charts course for its newest aviation adventure. AirAsia India, a JV with popular, low-cost airline AirAsia Berhad, begins operations. Read More Chairman Emeritus Ratan Tata signs a photo of JRD Tata on an AirAsia aircraft unveiled with JRD livery, in 2015. / To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that supports HTML5 video 2015 The group follows up that development with Vistara, Tata Sons' JV with Singapore Airlines. It celebrates flying 1 million customers that same year. Vistara Salutes JRD with Retrojet / To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that supports HTML5 video 2017 N. Chandrasekaran, CEO and MD of TCS since 2009, is appointed Chairman of Tata Sons by the Board of Directors. N. Chandrasekaran Appointed Chairman 2017 The Tata group is the new title sponsor of Asia\u2019s most prestigious marathon for 10 years, the Mumbai Marathon. It is jointly sponsored by Tata group and TCS. Tata Mumbai Marathon: Going The Distance 2017 TCS and Cornell Tech inaugurate the Tata Innovation Center to promote joint academic and industry research, following a $50 million investment by TCS. Read More Chairman N. Chandrasekaran and Chairman Emeritus Ratan N. Tata inaugurate the Tata Innovation Center in 2017. / To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that supports HTML5 video 2018 Tata Steel and Thyssenkrupp sign a landmark JV to create a new, strong pan-European steel champion that is structurally robust and competitive. Tata Steel and Thyssenkrupp sign JV 2018 TCS becomes the first listed Indian IT company to breach $100 billion in market capitalisation, ushering in a new phase of growth for India Inc. / 2019 | The Expansion Years In May 2019, Tata Global Beverages and Tata Chemicals announce transaction to combine their consumer brands and create a new entity, Tata Consumer Products Ltd. TGBL and Tata Chemicals' announcement To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that supports HTML5 video 2019 | The Expansion Years Brand Finance names Tata as India's most valuable brand for 2019. With its brand value growing by 37 percent to $19.6 billion, it is the fastest growing brand among the top 25. Tata is India's most valuable brand: Brand Finance / To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that supports HTML5 video 2020 | The Next 50 In January, Tata Motors announced the launch of India's Own Electric SUV, Nexon EV, ushering in a new wave of e-mobility in the country. Backed by Tata uniEVerse, the company is poised to create a holistic EV ecosystem to accelerate the adoption of EVs in India. Tata Motors Launches Nexon EV 2020 | The Next 50 The Tata group committed Rs 1500 crore in the fight against Covid-19. Tata companies and the Trusts swung into action, helping fulfil immediate needs of healthcare equipment and hospitals, as well as pursuing long-term efforts to keep the economy and country running. Tata group's response to Covid-19 / 2021 | The Next 50 Tata Digital Ltd, a 100% owned subsidiary of Tata Sons, acquires majority stakes in e-commerce grocery giant, BigBasket (in May), and online healthcare marketplace, 1MG (in June). It also invests in CureFit Healthcare. Tata Digital acquires majority stake in BigBasket 2021 | The Next 50 68 years after the Centre took over operations for Air India, the airline returned to the group after Tata Sons won a bid in October this year. The group took over management and control of the airline on January 27, 2022. Tata group to acquire 100% stake in Air India / To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that supports HTML5 video 2022 | The Next 50 The Tata group took over as title sponsor for the Indian Premier League\u2019s 2022 and 2023 seasons. The announcement was made in January. #DeshKaPitch To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that supports HTML5 video 2022 | The Next 50 Tata Digital Ltd, a 100% owned subsidiary of Tata Sons, launched its highly anticipated super-app, Tata Neu in April, seamlessly blending product commerce, service commerce, and financial services into a consumer-first, future-ready, integrated experience. India logs into Tata Neu 2022 | The Next 50 In November, Tata group announced it will consolidate its airlines Air India and Vistara, making it India\u2019s largest international carrier and second largest domestic carrier. Tata group to consolidate Air India and Vistara / 2022 Tata group affirms its aspiration to be net zero by 2045 and the vision of securing the future by innovating today. Project Aalingana / 2023 | The Next 50 Announces plans to build a 40GW battery cell gigafactory in the United Kingdom (UK). The investment, of over \u00a34 billion, will deliver electric mobility and renewable energy storage solutions Press Release read more Chairman N Chandrasekaran with the then Prime Minister of UK Rishi Sunak at the facility 2023 | The Next 50 The Tata group returns to the bourses after a gap of 12 years when Tata Technologies debuts with a record-breaking IPO The Making of a Blockbuster IPO read more Tata Technologies' leadership at the IPO ceremony / 2024 | The Next 50 The Tata group announces India's first indigenous semiconductor assembly and test facility in Assam, and the first semiconductor fab in Gujarat Press Release / Pause Play 1868 29-year-old Jamsetji Nusserwanji Tata starts a trading company with a capital of \u20b921,000 \u2014 the early beginnings of what would one day become the Tata group. Jamsetji Tata: The Giant Who Touched Tomorrow 1868 29-year-old Jamsetji Nusserwanji Tata starts a trading company with a capital of \u20b921,000 \u2014 the early beginnings of what would one day become the Tata group. Jamsetji Tata: The Giant Who Touched Tomorrow", "lists": [["1868", "1874", "1886", "1892", "1903", "1904", "1907", "1909", "1910", "1912", "1917", "1919", "1920", "1929", "1932", "1936", "1938", "1939", "1941", "1945", "1946", "1952", "1954", "1962", "1968", "1969", "1982", "1983", "1984", "1991", "1992", "1994", "1998", "2000", "2001", "2002", "2003", "2004", "2006", "2007", "2008", "2011", "2012", "2014", "2015", "2017", "2018", "2019", "2020", "2021", "2022", "2022", "2023", "2024"], [""], [""], [""], [""], [""], [""], ["", ""], [""], [""], ["", ""], [""], [""], [""], [""], ["", "", ""], [""], [""], [""], [""], ["", ""], [""], [""], ["", ""], ["", ""], [""], [""], [""], [""], [""], ["", "", ""], ["", ""], [""], [""], [""], [""], [""], [""], [""], ["", ""], [""], [""], [""], [""], ["", ""], [""], ["", "", ""], ["", ""], ["", ""], ["", ""], ["", ""], ["", "", ""], [""], ["", ""], [""]], "tables": [], "code_blocks": [], "meta_description": "From 1868 to the present. A timeline of the heritage and milestones of the Tata group.", "meta_keywords": "Heritage Stories,Heritage", "og_image": "https://www.tata.com/content/dam/tata/images/heritage/desktop/mills_heritage_card_desktop_362x362.jpg", "js_rendered": false, "js_message": "", "success": true, "error": "